# Anything listed here never reaches the build context, so it cannot end up in
# an image layer. This matters more than .gitignore does: a file deleted in a
# later build step is still recoverable from the layer that added it.
#
# This is an allow-list. Everything is left out, then the code the agent runs
# is brought back by name. The folder this is built from holds more than the
# repository does: a live deployment keeps backups, git bundles, notes and
# logs beside the code, and a list of things to leave out misses whatever
# nobody thought to name. A new folder of code has to be added below, and
# forgetting it fails loudly, as a missing module at start-up.

*

# The code the agent runs
!*.py
!core
!sources
!templates
!requirements.txt

# Private files that can sit inside the folders brought back above. Each
# pattern starts with **/ so it matches at any depth: a plain .env would
# match only at the top and let core/.env through.

# Secrets
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.bak*
**/*.pem
**/*.p12
**/*.key
**/*.token

# Runtime and personal data. The database, the CV and the company list are
# mounted at run time instead. The code reads no data file of these types
# from inside the image, so every one of them is left out.
**/data
**/logs
**/output
**/cache
**/config
**/resumes
**/linkedin profile
**/*.json
**/*.yaml
**/*.yml
**/*.txt
**/*.log
**/*.db
**/*.db-*
**/*.sqlite*
**/*.pdf
**/*.docx
**/*.bundle

# Local tooling and build noise
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# requirements.txt is a .txt file, so it is brought back once more after the
# rule above that leaves out every .txt.
!requirements.txt
